{"id": "sevt_01MHchgnnzGjUXyqgFNoeCgC", "content": [{"text": "The checkout deploy lines up with the spike, and the diff and logs confirm the mechanism.\n\n- **Deploy vs. metric:** Deploy `a3f9c21` (checkout, \"refactor order summary builder\") went out at 14:31:18. Checkout p99 was about 60\u201370 ms until 14:31. It was 347 ms at 14:32, 852 ms at 14:33, and 2\u20133.6 s from 14:36 onward.\n- **Diff:** The refactor replaced one batched `order_id__in=[...]` query for all order items with a per-order `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.id)` inside a loop. That is the N+1 pattern: one query per open order.\n- **Logs:** `app.log` has 30,964 `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries from checkout. The first appears at 14:31:18.144, the moment of the deploy. A single request (`req_030ba30eb081`) issues several of them back to back.\n- **DB pool:** Checkout `db_pool_utilization` rose from about 0.15 to 0.34 at 14:32. It reached 1.0 (saturated) at 14:37 and stayed there. That fits the extra queries tying up connections, so requests queue behind them.\n- **Ruled out:**\n  - The inventory deploy `7b3e8f0` landed at 14:55, after the spike began.\n  - The cart and auth deploys were hours earlier.\n  - The auth idp timeouts in the log start at 14:15, before the spike, and are unrelated to it.\n\nI didn't check cart, auth or inventory pool utilization, since a deploy lined up. I also didn't confirm that fixing the query brings p99 back down.\n\nThe fix is to revert `a3f9c21`, or restore the batched query and keep the readable loop for the grouping step.\n\n> **Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 the refactor turned one batched order-items query into one query per order, which flooded and saturated checkout's DB pool.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:54:31.481072Z", "type": "agent.message"}
